--------------------------------------------------------------------\nSteamIndex (https://www.steamindex.com/locomag/lcwr43.htm)\nciteturn122search10 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 3.2 years ago; \nLocomotive Magazine and Railway Carriage and Wagon Review\nVolume 43 (1937)\nKey file\n\nNumber 533 (15 January 1937)\n\nWho shall build?. 1-2\nBuilding standard lociomotives and rolling stock should lower costs: considers transfer of such construction from the railway companies to the contract suppliers who would gain through scale aqnd increase their overseas competiveness.  \n\nLondon & North Eastern Railway. 2\nThe first five of 17 A4 Pacific locomotives under construction at Doncaster were about to be placed in service. In view of their streamlined form it has been decided to name them after birds and the first five will be numbered from 4482 onward and called Golden Eagle, Falcon, Merlin, Kingfisher, Kestrel, thus associating them with the most graceful form.of speed and movement—that of the flight of birds. On January 6 No. 4482 Golden Eagle worked the 13.20 train out of King's Cross as far as Newcastle. This was one of the heaviest trains of the day, the gross load being never less than 500 tons. The new 2-6-0 locomotive now under construction at Darlington for service on the West Highland line is to be named Loch Long, and No. 3441 has been allocated to it. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nBusiness History Review: Volume 37 - | Cambridge Core (https://www.cambridge.org/core/journals/business-history-review/volume/192AFF023A42C72EE2895923F0706FF1?pageNum=4)\nciteturn122search11 [wordlim: 200] Published: 63.1 years ago; Crawled: 2 days ago; \n#### Other\n\n[Input] Select Announcement of Special Fashion Exhibition\n\n  * ### Announcement of Special Fashion Exhibition\n\n  *   *   * Published online by Cambridge University Press:\n    24 July 2012, pp. 268-278\n  *     * Article\n    *       * Get access\n\nCheck if you